  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Square Roots of Decimals and Approximation",
    "description": "Handle decimal places carefully when finding exact or approximate square roots.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the idea, derive the method, practice transfer, and use targeted remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5 “Square Roots and Cube Roots,” printed pages 180–205. Approved scope: Teach exact square roots of terminating decimals by grouping decimal digits in pairs and using equivalent fractions or place-value scaling. Explain that adding a zero can make the number of decimal places even without changing its value, and that the root has half as many decimal places when the decimal is a perfect square. For non-square decimals, use digit-pair long division to obtain an approximation and state its precision explicitly. Distinguish exact equality from rounded approximations; show checks by squaring bounds. Cover examples such as √0.0009=0.03, √175.2976=13.24, and approximating √3 or √0.9 to a specified number of decimal places. Learners should avoid shifting the decimal by the wrong number of places."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Decimal roots depend on place value. Pair decimal digits as carefully as whole-number digits, and distinguish an exact terminating root from a rounded approximation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For an exact terminating decimal, group decimal digits in pairs. For 0.0009, the four decimal places form two pairs and the root has two decimal places: √0.0009=0.03 because 0.03²=0.0009.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
